Which transport to use is one question: who are the two peers, and what can each of
them open? The session that runs on top is identical either way, so the choice is
only about how the bytes get from one peer to the other. This page walks the
decision and then spells out where two transports overlap on purpose.

## First, the one distinction that trips people up

A transport moves bytes **directly** between two peers, over a channel each end
opens for itself: a TCP socket, a QUIC endpoint, a WebRTC DataChannel. The
[relay]($link.page('relay')) is **not** a transport. It is a byte bridge for a peer
that cannot open a channel of its own, which in practice means a browser. So the
first question is not "which transport" but "can both peers open a channel?"

- Both peers can open a socket: pick a transport below and dial directly.
- One peer is a browser: it has no socket, so it either reaches a native peer
  **through the relay**, or holds a **direct WebRTC DataChannel** to another
  browser. More on that pairing below.

## If both peers can open a channel

| You have | Reach for | Because |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Two hosts on a network, and one is a [Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer | `tcp` | it speaks the Scheme object-capability system pancake interoperates with, on its cleartext wire byte for byte |
| Two hosts, and you want a stable identity that survives a restart | `keyed-tcp` | the name you dial (the *designator*) is the peer's public key, so another peer cannot claim your identity and you can re-dial the same one after a restart |
| Both peers in one process, or a test | `inproc` | in-memory pipes, no socket, no port to bind |
| Two hosts that want an encrypted channel, or that sit behind NAT | `iroh` | encrypted QUIC, dial-by-key, and discovery across the open internet |
| Many connections from one host without a thread apiece | `iroh`, or `tcp` driven by its completion loop | one readiness loop drives them all |

If you just want "a real network connection to a peer you can address," `tcp` is the
default. Reach past it when you need one specific thing it does not give you: a key
identity (`keyed-tcp`), encryption or NAT traversal (`iroh`), or no socket at all
(`inproc`).

One thing the default does *not* give you: `tcp` and `keyed-tcp` are **cleartext**,
with no encryption on the wire. There is no TLS-over-TCP transport today, so "I just
want an encrypted socket" currently means `iroh`, whose QUIC channel is encrypted (at
the cost of linking its Rust sidecar). You do not have to decide this up front, though:
because the session on top is identical, moving from `tcp` to `iroh` later changes only
the locator you dial, not your application. Start on `tcp`, switch when you need the
channel confidential.

## If a browser is involved

A browser opens no listening socket and dials no raw TCP or QUIC, so it never uses a
socket transport directly. It has two ways to reach the other side, and they are
complementary, not competing.

- **The relay** carries the browser's keyed session over a WebSocket to a peer that
  *does* have a socket. The relay dials the far side over `tcp` or `iroh` and pipes
  the two channels together, staying blind to the session
  ([ADR-0086](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0086-byte-transparent-relay.md)).
  Use it whenever a browser must reach a **native** peer, and as the dependable path
  between two browsers when a direct channel cannot form.
- **WebRTC** opens a **direct** DataChannel between two peers, most usefully two
  browsers, with no server on the byte path once it is up. It needs a signaling
  capability to exchange the setup (a room, or any object both peers can reach), and
  it needs ICE to find a path between the two ends. Use it when you want two browser
  peers talking directly and the setup can succeed.

So the browser rule of thumb: **a direct WebRTC DataChannel when it can form and you
want the peers direct; the relay when a browser must reach a native peer, or as the
fallback when WebRTC cannot.** The relay always works as long as a relay is reachable;
WebRTC removes the relay from the byte path but depends on signaling and connectivity.
Weigh the cost before offering both: WebRTC links its own Rust sidecar (needs `cargo`
to build), and crossing real NAT means standing up a STUN server. If you are not going
browser-to-browser, the relay alone is less to operate.

## The relay bridges channels; it does not translate the protocol

The relay translates
**channels**: it turns a browser's WebSocket into a far peer's TCP or QUIC stream and
moves bytes between them. It does **not** translate the protocol, parse a frame, or
hold a capability. That is why a browser can reach any socket transport through one
relay, and why the relay is never party to the session it carries. [The
relay]($link.page('relay')) covers that byte-transparency in full.

## Where transports overlap

A few pairs look redundant at a glance. Each pair is a different trade, not
duplication.

- **`keyed-tcp` and `iroh` both name a peer by its key.** `keyed-tcp` is plain
  cleartext sockets with a key designator: no encryption, no Rust sidecar, and a
  designator that survives a restart. `iroh` adds an encrypted QUIC channel, NAT
  traversal, and discovery, at the cost of linking the sidecar. Choose `keyed-tcp`
  when you want a stable key identity on an ordinary socket and nothing more; choose
  `iroh` when you want the channel encrypted and reachable across NAT. Same key-as-name
  idea, different channel underneath.
- **`iroh` and `webrtc` are both encrypted peer-to-peer channels.** What splits
  them is what each peer can run. A browser cannot open a QUIC endpoint or link
  iroh's native sidecar, so its only direct encrypted channel is a WebRTC
  DataChannel. Two native hosts reach for `iroh` instead, because it finds a path by
  discovery and dial-by-key with no external signaling, where WebRTC needs a
  signaling capability and ICE to pair. So `webrtc` is the browser's peer-to-peer
  channel and `iroh` is the native one, and they meet only when a native peer talks
  directly to a browser.
- **`tcp` and `keyed-tcp` are the same sockets, addressed differently.** `tcp`
  addresses a peer by `host:port` and speaks Goblins' wire; `keyed-tcp` addresses it
  by key. If you are talking to Goblins or to a fixed address, `tcp`; if you want the
  identity to be the key rather than the address, `keyed-tcp`.

The relay ships a reference implementation in every binding runtime (Deno, which is
`@pancake/relay`, plus Python, Rust, and Racket) for the same reason: an app written in one of
them gets a working relay without dragging in a foreign runtime. One test
matrix keeps the four relays identical.

Whichever you pick, the session and every capability on top of it are identical;
only the path the bytes take changes, which is why you can defer the decision and
change it later. The [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) and
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) pages cover their mechanics in depth, and
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the locators, designators, and handoffs
they all share.
